**Read:** [Mineralogy — Dexter Perkins, open editions](../sources/Mineralogy-Perkins.lingua#r_3Y3EJ93EG4V7Q0TZBCRRXVNVKP), all pyroxene/amphibole and chain-silicate sections; [Optical Mineralogy — Brock University course and laboratory material](../sources/Optical-Mineralogy-Brock.lingua#r_4HWN93T5EATAS40HNJGMCHSM7E), the complete pyroxene and amphibole optical/laboratory material; [Mineral names and classification reports — IMA-CNMNC](../sources/IMA-CNMNC-Mineral-Names-and-Reports.lingua#r_1B4ZWGSZKGP6JM3NKPY0D5RJXH), the selected full nomenclature reports; [Handbook of Mineralogy — Anthony, Bideaux, Bladh, and Nichols](../sources/Handbook-of-Mineralogy.lingua#r_10TEF3RSN7XFMKTGYGY4WR3P85), complete selected entries.

Single-chain pyroxenes and double-chain amphiboles connect silicate topology with distinct site arrangements and compositional possibilities. Their common cleavage patterns help identification under appropriate views, but cleavage alone is not a complete chemical or species diagnosis. Grain orientation and alteration influence what can be seen.

Study orthopyroxene, clinopyroxene, and representative amphiboles with site formulas and compositional variation. A quadrilateral or group name is a projection into selected components, not the entire chemistry of every natural analysis. Ferric iron, alkalis, aluminium, titanium, hydrogen, and other components can matter.

Optical properties can support a group identification while leaving exact composition or species unresolved. A chemical normalization needs the relevant oxygen/anion basis, valence assumptions, and site-allocation model. An analysis spanning exsolution lamellae can describe a mixture rather than one homogeneous phase.

**Work:** compare a pyroxene and amphibole from hand specimen through thin section and a complete published analysis. Explain the structural/charge account, optical evidence, uncertainty, and nomenclature report used. Test whether a simplified endmember projection preserves the information needed for the proposed rock-history inference.

Read the complete chain-silicate chapters and optical labs. Later thermobarometry and igneous/metamorphic studies will demand a defensible mineral-composition and equilibrium account before translating these minerals into pressure, temperature, or source history.

